Introduction: The Fragmentation of Mass and the Necessity of a Contextual Operator
Classical mechanics, general relativity, quantum field theory, and fluid dynamics operate on fundamentally incompatible definitions of mass. In classical mechanics, mass is an invariant scalar measure of inertia (F = ma). In general relativity, it acts as the source term for spacetime curvature within the Einstein field equations (G_{\mu\nu} = \frac{8\pi G}{c^4} T_{\mu\nu}). In quantum field theory, particle mass emerges dynamically through spontaneous symmetry breaking via Yukawa couplings to the vacuum expectation value of the Higgs field. In fluid dynamics and magnetohydrodynamics, mass density operates as a continuous field parameter driving momentum conservation and shock propagation.
These frameworks have historically treated mass as disparate manifestations of distinct physical mechanisms. This fragmentation breaks down when evaluating extreme astrophysical environments—such as magnetar crusts, relativistic jets, accretion disks around Kerr-Newman black holes, and deep interior quark-gluon plasmas—where inertial resistance, metric curvature, electromagnetic field pressure, and plasma density interact simultaneously.
To resolve this multi-regime anomaly, we introduce the Contextual Mass-Emergence Tensor (CMET-SE). By replacing static rest-scalars with a unified tensor operator \mathcal{M}_{\mu\nu}(x^\alpha), this framework bridges the gap between microscopic field interactions and macroscopic cosmological telemetry, providing a fully falsifiable foundation for unified mass-energy dynamics.
